    "q-167316": {
      "dom_id": "question-167316",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=x\\cdot f(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate using the product rule. \n\nIn part 2 we found that $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}+f\\frac{\\text{d}(x)}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(2-3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(3+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =(2-3(0))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+(3+2(0))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
