    "q-167317": {
      "dom_id": "question-167317",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
      "worked_solution": "When $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0\\cdot f(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is $m=3.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
